WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.722
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:06.022 --> 00:00:09.071
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.371 --> 00:00:16.424
Unit twenty-three: Digital Payments. Today you will express preferences with would rather, prefer and had better.

4
00:00:16.724 --> 00:00:19.225
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.330
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.183 --> 00:00:25.277
Digital payment.

7
00:00:26.283 --> 00:00:29.804
Digital payment means paying with electronic methods instead of cash.

8
00:00:30.417 --> 00:00:32.997
A digital payment takes only seconds.

9
00:00:33.583 --> 00:00:36.712
Your turn. Say: Digital payment.

10
00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:40.788
Digital payment.

11
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:42.574
QR code.

12
00:00:43.583 --> 00:00:47.359
QR code means a square pattern of dots that a phone can scan.

13
00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:49.822
Scan the QR code to pay.

14
00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:53.522
Your turn. Say: QR code.

15
00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:57.403
QR code.

16
00:00:58.150 --> 00:00:59.372
Mobile wallet.

17
00:01:00.383 --> 00:01:03.950
Mobile wallet means an app that stores payment information on a phone.

18
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:06.898
She keeps her cards in a mobile wallet.

19
00:01:07.483 --> 00:01:10.089
Your turn. Say: Mobile wallet.

20
00:01:12.933 --> 00:01:14.213
Mobile wallet.

21
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:15.684
Bank.

22
00:01:16.683 --> 00:01:20.285
Bank means an institution that keeps money safe and lends it.

23
00:01:20.883 --> 00:01:23.069
The bank introduced a new payment app.

24
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.941
Your turn. Say: Bank.

25
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:29.627
Bank.

26
00:01:30.383 --> 00:01:31.233
Cash.

27
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.155
Cash means money in the form of coins and notes.

28
00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:38.284
Many street sellers still prefer cash.

29
00:01:38.883 --> 00:01:41.118
Your turn. Say: Cash.

30
00:01:43.967 --> 00:01:44.921
Cash.

31
00:01:45.683 --> 00:01:46.719
Security.

32
00:01:47.717 --> 00:01:51.771
Security means protection against theft, fraud or danger.

33
00:01:52.383 --> 00:01:55.358
Security is the biggest concern in online payments.

34
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.637
Your turn. Say: Security.

35
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.350
Security.

36
00:02:03.117 --> 00:02:04.025
Fees.

37
00:02:05.017 --> 00:02:07.504
Fees means money charged for a service.

38
00:02:08.117 --> 00:02:10.232
Some apps charge small fees.

39
00:02:10.817 --> 00:02:13.086
Your turn. Say: Fees.

40
00:02:15.933 --> 00:02:16.575
Fees.

41
00:02:17.317 --> 00:02:18.329
Transaction.

42
00:02:19.317 --> 00:02:23.371
Transaction means an act of buying, selling or transferring money.

43
00:02:23.983 --> 00:02:26.308
Each transaction is recorded instantly.

44
00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:29.534
Your turn. Say: Transaction.

45
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.471
Transaction.

46
00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:38.115
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.006
Visal, do you prefer cash or digital payments?

48
00:02:43.117 --> 00:02:53.641
I'd rather pay digitally, because I prefer using a QR code to carrying coins. However, I'd rather not use public Wi-Fi for a transaction.

49
00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.204
What is the grammar there?

50
00:02:57.317 --> 00:03:08.839
'Would rather + base verb' states a preference. 'Prefer + -ing' or 'prefer A to B' is another pattern: I prefer paying digitally to paying with cash.

51
00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:11.160
And advice?

52
00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:21.938
You'd better check the fees and security settings. 'Had better' gives strong advice and, like would rather, takes the base verb.

53
00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.658
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.517 --> 00:03:30.488
Grammar focus: Preferences and advice: would rather and prefer and had better.

55
00:03:30.788 --> 00:03:31.987
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.683 --> 00:03:34.509
I would rather pay with my phone.

57
00:03:35.417 --> 00:03:38.322
She prefers paying digitally to paying with cash.

58
00:03:39.217 --> 00:03:41.239
You had better check the fees first.

59
00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:53.133
Would rather + base verb (no 'to'): I'd rather pay. Prefer + -ing, or prefer A to B. Had better + base verb for strong advice; negative: had better not.

60
00:03:53.883 --> 00:03:57.290
Be careful! Don't say: You had better to check fees.

61
00:03:59.233 --> 00:04:01.407
Say: You had better check fees.

62
00:04:01.707 --> 00:04:03.799
Use the base verb after had better.

63
00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.264
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:08.067 --> 00:04:09.230
Cash or Code?.

65
00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:13.968
Digital payment has spread quickly, from the bank app to the QR code on a street stall.

66
00:04:14.967 --> 00:04:20.623
Many customers would rather use a mobile wallet than carry cash, because each transaction takes seconds.

67
00:04:21.633 --> 00:04:27.278
Others prefer paying with cash to using an app, mainly because of security worries and fees.

68
00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:32.565
Experts say users had better turn on security settings and check every message.

69
00:04:33.567 --> 00:04:37.226
In practice, most people will use both for some years.

70
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:42.300
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:45.138
I would rather blank with my phone.

72
00:04:45.438 --> 00:04:48.906
A, pay. B, to pay. C, paying.

73
00:04:52.467 --> 00:04:54.327
The answer is A: pay.

74
00:04:55.483 --> 00:04:58.388
She prefers paying digitally blank paying with cash.

75
00:04:58.688 --> 00:05:01.309
A, to. B, than. C, from.

76
00:05:04.867 --> 00:05:06.611
The answer is A: to.

77
00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:10.353
Protection against theft or fraud is blank.

78
00:05:10.653 --> 00:05:14.794
A, security. B, a transaction. C, a fee.

79
00:05:18.333 --> 00:05:20.658
The answer is A: security.

80
00:05:21.817 --> 00:05:23.445
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:23.745 --> 00:05:30.080
A, You had better to check fees.. B, You had better check fees.. C, You had better checking fees..

82
00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:36.829
The answer is B: You had better check fees..

83
00:05:37.983 --> 00:05:40.668
According to the reading, most people will use…

84
00:05:40.968 --> 00:05:45.388
A, both for some years. B, only cash. C, only codes.

85
00:05:48.933 --> 00:05:51.699
The answer is A: both for some years.

86
00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:56.017
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:56.883 --> 00:05:59.954
Do you prefer cash or digital payments? Why?

88
00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:10.274
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:10.574 --> 00:06:31.412
I would rather pay digitally because it is fast and I do not need to carry money. A QR code or mobile wallet records each transaction, so it is easy to check my spending. However, I keep a little cash for places that do not accept apps. I also check the fees and security settings first.

90
00:06:31.712 --> 00:06:33.480
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:34.383 --> 00:06:40.542
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and preferences and advice: would rather and prefer and had better.

92
00:06:40.842 --> 00:06:45.224
Today's value is digital trust. Protect the trust that others place in you.

93
00:06:45.524 --> 00:06:48.269
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

94
00:06:48.569 --> 00:06:51.013
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

